T³ES 0.5 — Lunar Pit-Based Gravity Storage with In-Situ Mass Production

Pre-Feasibility Specification and Simulation Framework

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI: chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

This work presents a refined pre-feasibility specification for a lunar gravity energy storage system exploiting locally manufactured mass modules within an excavated vertical shaft. The central innovation is the recognition that the excavation process itself generates the storage medium: the regolith removed to create the storage volume is simultaneously processed into compacted mass modules that constitute the storage inventory. This dual-use excavation strategy transforms what would otherwise be waste material into the primary energy storage asset, eliminating the prohibitive Earth-imported mass penalty that has historically excluded gravity storage from lunar mission architectures.

The system is evaluated through three complementary metrics: Earth-Imported Mass Efficiency (EIME), quantifying lifetime usable energy per kilogram of Earth-imported mass; Local Resource Energy Efficiency (LREE), quantifying the energy payback relative to in-situ construction energy; and Autonomous Infrastructure Efficiency (AIE), which unifies imported mass and local energy into a single figure of merit through an infrastructure-equivalence coefficient. This multi-metric framework prevents the optimistic bias that arises when locally sourced mass is treated as energetically free while simultaneously capturing the genuine advantage of ISRU-based storage.

Under corrected baseline assumptions—including mechanical degradation integrated over the full cycle life, realistic excavation energy derived from lunar geotechnical studies, and explicit accounting of geotextile containment mass—the single-module system stores 675 kWh of potential energy (448.9 kWh usable at 66.5% initial round-trip efficiency) using 2,000 compacted regolith modules of 5,000 kg each within a 150 m shaft. The analysis demonstrates that the EIME advantage over electrochemical alternatives, while reduced from initial estimates, remains potentially significant under conservative assumptions, with the system achieving approximately 1,600–1,800 kWh/kg compared to 360 kWh/kg for lithium-ion and 1,250 kWh/kg for regenerative fuel cells. However, this advantage is contingent upon validation of cycle life, mechanical degradation rates, and in-situ bag production feasibility, which constitute the primary objectives of the proposed simulation campaign.

The document provides a complete mathematical framework, simulation-ready specification, and research roadmap for systematic validation of the concept. No physical prototyping is claimed; all results remain pending the computational campaign defined herein.

