1. Introduction and Design Rationale

1.1 The Storage Challenge on the Lunar Surface

Lunar surface operations face a fundamental energy storage challenge: the 29.5-Earth-day synodic cycle imposes a ~354-hour night during which photovoltaic generation is unavailable. For a sustained lunar presence, the storage system must provide continuous power throughout this period while minimizing the mass that must be transported from Earth at prohibitive cost. Current candidate technologies—lithium-ion batteries and regenerative fuel cells—both require the entire storage medium to be imported, imposing a direct proportionality between required capacity and launch mass.

1.2 The Gravity Storage Opportunity

Gravity energy storage on the Moon offers a unique advantage: the storage medium can, in principle, be manufactured entirely from lunar regolith using in-situ resource utilization (ISRU) techniques. The fundamental energy storage equation

E_g = m g h

with lunar gravity g = 1.62\ \text{m/s}^2, mass m, and height h, becomes economically viable only when m is locally sourced. The challenge is therefore not the physics of energy storage, but the engineering of mass production and mechanical handling in the lunar environment.

1.3 The Dual-Use Excavation Principle

The architectural innovation presented here is the recognition that the storage volume and the storage medium are co-generated: excavating a vertical shaft produces both the height differential required for gravitational storage and the raw material for manufacturing the storage masses. This eliminates the need for separate mining operations and transforms the excavation from a pure construction cost into a simultaneous construction and manufacturing process.

1.4 Multi-Metric Evaluation Framework

Traditional evaluation of storage technologies for space applications has focused on energy density and round-trip efficiency. This work argues that these metrics are incomplete for ISRU-based systems. Three complementary metrics are defined:

Earth-Imported Mass Efficiency (EIME):

\text{EIME} = \frac{E_{\text{usable,lifetime}}}{M_{\text{imported}}}
\quad \left[ \frac{\text{kWh}}{\text{kg}_{\text{Earth}}} \right]

This metric captures the fundamental launch-cost constraint.

Local Resource Energy Efficiency (LREE):

\text{LREE} = \frac{E_{\text{usable,lifetime}}}{E_{\text{local,construction}}}

This metric captures the energy payback of the in-situ manufacturing process.

Autonomous Infrastructure Efficiency (AIE):

\text{AIE} = \frac{E_{\text{usable,lifetime}}}{M_{\text{imported}} + \lambda E_{\text{local,construction}}}

where \lambda converts local construction energy into an equivalent imported-mass penalty, reflecting the infrastructure cost of generating that energy.

