2. Fundamental Physics and Mathematical Models

2.1 Gravitational Energy Storage

The potential energy stored per mass module of mass m elevated through height h is

E_b = m g h

with g = 1.62\ \text{m/s}^2 on the lunar surface. For a batch of N_b modules handled simultaneously, the stored energy is

E_{\text{batch}} = N_b m g h.

The mechanical power during controlled descent at velocity v is

P_{\text{mech}} = N_b m g v,

and the electrical output after generation and power conditioning is

P_{\text{out}} = \eta_{\text{gen}} \eta_{\text{PE}} N_b m g v,

where \eta_{\text{gen}} is generator efficiency and \eta_{\text{PE}} is power-electronics efficiency.

2.2 Round-Trip Efficiency Model

The round-trip efficiency is the product of component efficiencies:

\eta_{\text{RT}} = \eta_{\text{motor}} \cdot \eta_{\text{mech,up}} \cdot \eta_{\text{mech,down}} \cdot \eta_{\text{generator}} \cdot \eta_{\text{PE}}.

Baseline component efficiencies:

\eta_{\text{motor}} = 0.92, \quad \eta_{\text{generator}} = 0.92, \quad \eta_{\text{PE}} = 0.97,

\eta_{\text{mech,up}}^{(0)} = 0.90, \quad \eta_{\text{mech,down}}^{(0)} = 0.90.

Initial round-trip efficiency:

\eta_{\text{RT}}^{(0)} = 0.92 \times 0.90 \times 0.90 \times 0.92 \times 0.97 \approx 0.665.

2.3 Mechanical Degradation Model

Lunar dust abrasion and vacuum cold-welding effects are modeled as exponential decay of mechanical efficiency:

\eta_{\text{mech}}(n) = \eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} \cdot r^{n/1000},

where n is the cumulative cycle count and r is the retention factor per 1,000 cycles. The simulation framework treats r as a parametric variable with baseline r = 0.95 and sensitivity range r \in [0.90, 0.99].

The average mechanical efficiency over N cycles is

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} \cdot \frac{1 - r^{N/1000}}{(N/1000) \ln(1/r)}.

For N = 10{,}000 and r = 0.95:

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} \approx 0.90 \times 0.782 = 0.704.

The average round-trip efficiency over system life is therefore

\bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}} \approx 0.92 \times 0.704 \times 0.704 \times 0.92 \times 0.97 \approx 0.406.

This is substantially lower than the initial efficiency and must be integrated into all lifetime energy calculations.

2.4 Mass Module Production Energy

The energy required to produce one compacted regolith mass module is decomposed as

E_{\text{block}} = E_{\text{excavation}} + E_{\text{transport}} + E_{\text{processing}} + E_{\text{bagging}} + E_{\text{forming}}.

Based on lunar geotechnical studies, the excavation energy for regolith is approximately

e_{\text{excavation}} \approx 20\ \text{kJ/kg} = 0.00556\ \text{kWh/kg}.

For a 5,000 kg module, this yields

E_{\text{excavation,block}} = 5000 \times 0.00556 = 27.8\ \text{kWh}.

Additional processing (sieving, compaction, bagging) is estimated at 20% of excavation energy:

E_{\text{processing,block}} \approx 5.56\ \text{kWh},

giving total production energy per module:

E_{\text{block,total}} \approx 33.4\ \text{kWh}.

This is significantly higher than previous estimates that neglected the full excavation energy.

2.5 Energy Payback Ratio (EPR)

The energy payback ratio, defined as lifetime usable energy divided by total construction energy, is

\text{EPR} = \frac{E_{\text{usable}} \cdot N_{\text{cycles}} \cdot \bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}}}{E_{\text{production,total}} + E_{\text{imported,embedded}}}.

For the baseline configuration:

E_{\text{usable}} = 675 \times 0.665 = 448.9\ \text{kWh},

N_{\text{cycles}} = 10{,}000,

\bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}} \approx 0.406,

E_{\text{production,total}} = 2000 \times 33.4 = 66{,}800\ \text{kWh}.

Assuming imported embedded energy is negligible compared to local construction energy:

\text{EPR} \approx \frac{448.9 \times 10{,}000 \times 0.406}{66{,}800} \approx 27.3.

This indicates the system returns approximately 27 times more energy over its lifetime than was invested in its construction—a favourable energy payback despite the increased excavation energy.

