3. System Architecture

3.1 Single Pit Module

The reference architecture consists of one vertical shaft of depth h = 150 m located on the rim of a permanently shadowed region (PSR) at the lunar south pole. The rim position provides near-continuous solar illumination for the photovoltaic array, while the PSR offers a natural radiative cold sink for thermal management.

The shaft has a rectangular cross-section of approximately 20 \times 20 m, with walls stabilized by compacted regolith lining. The hoist mechanism is mounted at the surface, operating a batch carriage that handles N_b = 14 modules simultaneously.

3.2 Continuous Counterweight Architecture

To maximize energy efficiency, the system employs a continuous counterweight configuration rather than a simple single-carriage design. In this configuration, descending mass modules are mechanically coupled to ascending empty carriages, partially offsetting the gravitational load on the hoist motor.

```
        TOP SURFACE
    ┌─────────────────┐
    │   HOIST DRIVE   │
    │   MOTOR/GENERATOR│
    └───────┬─────────┘
            │
    ┌───────▼─────────┐
    │  CABLE LOOP     │
    │  (Zylon/Kevlar) │
    └───┬─────────┬───┘
        │         │
   ┌────▼───┐ ┌───▼────┐
   │DESCEND │ │ ASCEND │
   │ 14×5t  │ │ EMPTY  │
   └────┬───┘ └───┬────┘
        │         │
        ▼         ▼
    ┌─────────────────┐
    │   SHAFT         │
    │   (150 m)       │
    │                 │
    │  █ █ █ █ █ █ █  │
    │  █ █ █ █ █ █ █  │
    └─────────────────┘
```

3.3 Thermal Management

All waste heat from the motor/generator and power electronics is rejected radiatively. The radiator area required for a heat load \dot{Q} at temperature T_{\text{rad}} is

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{\dot{Q}}{\epsilon \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4},

with \epsilon = 0.9, \sigma = 5.67 \times 10^{-8}\ \text{W/m}^2\text{K}^4. For \dot{Q} = 10\ \text{kW} and T_{\text{rad}} = 300\ \text{K}:

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{10{,}000}{0.9 \times 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \times 300^4} \approx 24.1\ \text{m}^2.

At an areal density of 5 kg/m², the radiator mass is approximately 120 kg.

