4.2 Mass Module Production Specification

The mass modules are produced through the following sequence:

1. Excavation: The shaft excavation process generates regolith that is stockpiled at the surface.
2. Sieving: Particles larger than 1 cm are removed for separate processing or discarded.
3. Bag filling: Regolith is loaded into high-strength geotextile bags (Vectran or equivalent).
4. Compaction: Vibratory compaction achieves a density of approximately 1.8 g/cm³.
5. Sealing and stacking: Bags are sealed and positioned at the shaft bottom or top as required.

Production energy per module:

E_{\text{block,total}} = E_{\text{excavation}} + E_{\text{processing}}

E_{\text{block,total}} = 27.8 + 5.56 = 33.4\ \text{kWh}.

Total production energy for 2,000 modules:

E_{\text{production,total}} = 66{,}800\ \text{kWh}.

At a dedicated production power of 100 kW:

T_{\text{production}} = \frac{66{,}800}{100} = 668\ \text{h} \approx 27.8\ \text{Earth days}.

This is achievable within one lunar day (29.5 Earth days) if production runs continuously, but leaves minimal margin.

