8. Conclusion

The refined analysis demonstrates that lunar gravity energy storage with locally manufactured mass modules remains a potentially viable concept, though the corrected EIME values are more conservative than initial estimates. Under baseline assumptions with integrated mechanical degradation, the system achieves EIME parity with lithium-ion batteries and approximately 60% of regenerative fuel cell EIME. However, the fundamental advantage of the concept—decoupling storage capacity from Earth-imported mass—remains intact and becomes more significant as storage duration requirements increase beyond the single lunar night reference case.

The primary uncertainties that must be resolved through simulation are: (1) mechanical degradation rates under lunar conditions, (2) excavation energy requirements for 150 m shaft construction, (3) geotextile bag mass and UV stability, and (4) geotechnical stability of the shaft. Each of these factors directly influences the EIME and must be quantified before proceeding to multi-module scaling.

The simulation campaign outlined in Section 6 provides a systematic framework for resolving these uncertainties. The decision gate criteria in Section 7.2 establish clear quantitative thresholds for concept advancement, ensuring that resource allocation decisions are based on validated performance rather than optimistic assumptions.

The concept remains scientifically falsifiable: if the simulation results demonstrate that EIME cannot exceed the decision gate threshold even under optimistic parameter selections, the concept should be abandoned for lunar applications and potentially reconsidered for Mars, where higher gravity (g = 3.71\ \text{m/s}^2) provides 2.29 times greater energy density per unit mass and sulfur is more abundant for alternative consolidation methods.

