A.3 Mechanical Degradation Model

A.3.1 Exponential Decay Formulation

The mechanical efficiency degrades exponentially with cycle count due to dust abrasion, cold welding, and general wear:

\eta_{\text{mech}}(n) = \eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} \cdot r^{n/1000}

where:

· n = cycle count
· r = retention factor per 1,000 cycles
· \eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} = initial mechanical efficiency

Justification: The exponential model is chosen because wear mechanisms in vacuum environments typically follow Arrhenius-type degradation kinetics, where the rate of material removal is proportional to the current surface condition. This leads to exponential decay in performance metrics.

A.3.2 Average Efficiency Over System Life

The average mechanical efficiency over N cycles is:

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{1}{N} \int_0^N \eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} r^{n/1000} \, dn

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{\eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)}}{N} \int_0^N e^{n \ln(r)/1000} \, dn

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{\eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)}}{N} \cdot \frac{1000}{\ln(r)} \left[ e^{N \ln(r)/1000} - 1 \right]

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{\eta_{\text{mech}}^{(0)} \cdot 1000}{N \ln(r)} \left[ r^{N/1000} - 1 \right]

Since \ln(r) < 0 for r < 1, this expression is positive.

A.3.3 Numerical Evaluation for Baseline Parameters

For r = 0.95, N = 10{,}000:

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{0.90 \times 1000}{10{,}000 \times \ln(0.95)} \left[ 0.95^{10} - 1 \right]

\ln(0.95) = -0.05129

0.95^{10} = 0.5987

\bar{\eta}_{\text{mech}} = \frac{900}{-512.9} \times (-0.4013) = \frac{900 \times 0.4013}{512.9} = 0.704

Average Round-Trip Efficiency:

\bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}} = 0.92 \times 0.704 \times 0.704 \times 0.92 \times 0.97

\bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}} = 0.92^2 \times 0.704^2 \times 0.97 = 0.8464 \times 0.4956 \times 0.97

\bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}} = 0.406

