A.4 Excavation Energy Model

A.4.1 Geotechnical Basis

The energy required to excavate lunar regolith is derived from terrestrial and lunar analogue studies. The specific excavation energy depends on:

· Regolith cohesion
· Internal friction angle
· Bulk density
· Tool geometry
· Cutting depth
· Operating temperature

Reference Value:

Based on analyses of Apollo samples and lunar regolith simulants, the specific excavation energy for mechanical excavation is:

e_{\text{excavation}} \approx 20\ \text{kJ/kg} = 0.00556\ \text{kWh/kg}

A.4.2 Total Excavation Mass

The shaft dimensions determine the total excavated mass. For a rectangular shaft of width W = 20\ \text{m}, length L = 20\ \text{m}, and depth h = 150\ \text{m}:

V_{\text{shaft}} = W \times L \times h = 20 \times 20 \times 150 = 60{,}000\ \text{m}^3

At a regolith bulk density of \rho = 1.5\ \text{g/cm}^3 = 1{,}500\ \text{kg/m}^3:

M_{\text{excavated}} = V_{\text{shaft}} \times \rho = 60{,}000 \times 1{,}500 = 90{,}000{,}000\ \text{kg} = 90{,}000\ \text{tonnes}

This is significantly larger than the total mass module mass of 10,000 tonnes (2,000 modules × 5 tonnes). The excess excavated material (80,000 tonnes) must be either stockpiled on the surface, used for other construction, or spread in thin layers.

A.4.3 Total Excavation Energy

E_{\text{excavation,total}} = M_{\text{excavated}} \times e_{\text{excavation}}

E_{\text{excavation,total}} = 90{,}000{,}000 \times 0.00556 = 500{,}400\ \text{kWh}

This is the total energy for excavating the entire shaft, not just the energy for producing the mass modules.

A.4.4 Excavation Energy Allocation

The excavation energy can be allocated to:

1. Mass module production: The portion of excavated material used for modules (10,000 tonnes)
2. Shaft construction: The remaining 80,000 tonnes represents the shaft volume itself

Module allocation:

E_{\text{excavation,modules}} = 10{,}000{,}000 \times 0.00556 = 55{,}600\ \text{kWh}

Per module:

E_{\text{excavation,per module}} = \frac{55{,}600}{2000} = 27.8\ \text{kWh}

A.4.5 Processing Energy

Additional processing steps include sieving, transport, bag filling, and compaction. Based on terrestrial analogs and scaling for lunar gravity:

e_{\text{processing}} \approx 0.20 \times e_{\text{excavation}} = 0.20 \times 0.00556 = 0.00111\ \text{kWh/kg}

Per module:

E_{\text{processing,per module}} = 5000 \times 0.00111 = 5.56\ \text{kWh}

A.4.6 Total Production Energy Per Module

E_{\text{block,total}} = E_{\text{excavation}} + E_{\text{transport}} + E_{\text{processing}} + E_{\text{bagging}} + E_{\text{forming}}

Assuming transport, bagging, and forming are included within the processing estimate:

E_{\text{block,total}} = 27.8 + 5.56 = 33.36\ \text{kWh}

Rounding to three significant figures:

E_{\text{block,total}} \approx 33.4\ \text{kWh}

A.4.7 Total Production Energy

E_{\text{production,total}} = 2000 \times 33.4 = 66{,}800\ \text{kWh}

A.4.8 Production Time

At a dedicated production power of P_{\text{production}} = 100\ \text{kW}:

T_{\text{production}} = \frac{66{,}800}{100} = 668\ \text{h} = 27.8\ \text{Earth days}

At 50 kW:

T_{\text{production}} = \frac{66{,}800}{50} = 1{,}336\ \text{h} = 55.7\ \text{Earth days}

This exceeds one lunar day, indicating that at least 100 kW must be dedicated to production, or the process must span multiple lunar days.

A.5 Energy Payback Ratio (EPR)

A.5.1 Formal Definition

The energy payback ratio is defined as:

\text{EPR} = \frac{E_{\text{usable,lifetime}}}{E_{\text{invested,total}}}

where:

E_{\text{usable,lifetime}} = E_{\text{usable}} \times N_{\text{cycles}} \times \bar{\eta}_{\text{RT}}

and:

E_{\text{invested,total}} = E_{\text{production,total}} + E_{\text{imported,embedded}}

The imported embedded energy accounts for the energy required to manufacture and transport Earth-imported components. For simplicity, this is often assumed negligible compared to local construction energy for ISRU systems.

