Bag Thickness Calculation:

The required bag thickness for a 5,000 kg module with safety factor 5:

\sigma_{\text{hoop}} = \frac{P \times D}{2t}

where P is the internal pressure from the regolith, D is the bag diameter, and t is the wall thickness.

Assuming the bag is approximately spherical with radius r = 0.9\ \text{m}:

P = \rho g h_{\text{bag}} = 1{,}800 \times 1.62 \times 1.2 = 3{,}499\ \text{Pa}

t = \frac{P \times D \times \text{SF}}{2 \times \sigma_{\text{allowable}}}

t = \frac{3{,}499 \times 1.8 \times 5}{2 \times 3.0 \times 10^9} = \frac{31{,}491}{6 \times 10^9} = 5.25 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{m} = 5.25\ \mu\text{m}

This is extremely thin, indicating that the actual bag mass is limited by handling requirements rather than strength. A practical minimum thickness of 1 mm is assumed, giving:

A_{\text{bag}} = 4\pi r^2 = 4\pi \times 0.9^2 = 10.18\ \text{m}^2

V_{\text{bag material}} = A_{\text{bag}} \times t = 10.18 \times 0.001 = 0.01018\ \text{m}^3

M_{\text{bag}} = V_{\text{bag material}} \times \rho_{\text{Vectran}} = 0.01018 \times 1{,}400 = 14.25\ \text{kg}

This suggests the 3 kg bag mass estimate may be optimistic. A more realistic estimate is 5–15 kg per bag, depending on the design.

