B.6 Control System Architecture

B.6.1 State Machine

The control system implements a finite state machine with the following states:

1. IDLE — No active charging or discharging
2. CHARGING — Lifting batches from bottom to top
3. DISCHARGING — Lowering batches from top to bottom
4. FAULT — Emergency stop, mechanical brakes engaged
5. MAINTENANCE — Inspection, repair operations

B.6.2 Charging Logic

```
WHILE (solar power > load demand) AND (blocks at bottom > 0):
    1. Load carriage with 14 blocks from bottom storage
    2. Lift carriage at v = 1 m/s
    3. Unload blocks at top storage
    4. Return empty carriage at v = 2 m/s (faster, no load)
    5. Repeat
```

B.6.3 Discharging Logic

```
WHILE (load demand > solar power) AND (blocks at top > 0):
    1. Load carriage with 14 blocks from top storage
    2. Lower carriage at v = 1 m/s (generating power)
    3. Unload blocks at bottom storage
    4. Return empty carriage at v = 2 m/s
    5. Repeat
```

B.6.4 Emergency Stop

In the event of a fault:

1. Mechanical brakes engage (redundant, spring-loaded)
2. Motor/generator disconnects from power bus
3. Carriage is held in position
4. Fault code is logged
5. Operator (or autonomous system) determines recovery action

